# Adaptive Reasoning

## Overview

Your support desk gets order lookups, one-line policy questions, and requests
to compare options. `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive` picks one reasoning Flow per
request with a local keyword rule, so no model call is spent on routing. A
caller can also name the Flow:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
ask.(%{input: "Please look up order A-1001."}).data.flow
# => %{id: "jido.ai/reasoning/react", vsn: 1}

ask.(%{input: "Please look up order A-1001.", strategy: :chain_of_thought}).data.flow
# => %{id: "jido.ai/reasoning/chain-of-thought", vsn: 1}
```

You'll read the exact selection rule, run selected Flows offline, and override
the choice. Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Define the Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the status and delivery estimate for one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.string(), eta: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/support_agent", vsn: 1, name: "support_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end
```

The tools stay on the Agent. Only ReAct sends them to the model; the other
Flows ignore them.

## The Graph

```elixir
Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive.flow().components
|> Enum.map(fn {name, component} -> {name, component.kind} end)
```

The result is `[{"reason", :invoke}]`. One `invoke` component runs
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive.Actions.Select` as its decision and
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive.Actions.Expand` as its expander. `Select` returns
a strategy atom and the request input. `Expand` maps the atom to a module and
continues into that Flow. Everything after that is the selected Flow, so the
reply has its `flow`, `reasoning`, and result shape.

## The Selection Rule

With no `strategy`, `Select` lowercases the input, splits it on every
character other than `a-z`, `0-9`, and `-`, and tests the words in this order.
The first match wins:

| Order | Any word in | Flow |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `puzzle riddle iterate improve refine recursive` | `:trm` |
| 2 | `synthesize combine merge integrate relationships perspectives` | `:graph_of_thoughts` |
| 3 | `search find lookup fetch calculate compute execute run tool` | `:react` |
| 4 | `algorithm backtrack search-space optimize` | `:algorithm_of_thoughts` |
| 5 | `analyze explore compare evaluate alternatives options` | `:tree_of_thoughts` |
| 6 | Fewer than 12 words | `:chain_of_draft` |
| 7 | Anything else | `:chain_of_thought` |

Matching is by whole word. `look` and `lookups` don't match `lookup`, and a
multi-part input contributes only its `text` parts.

Run the decision Action directly to see the choice for each input:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Adaptive.Actions.Select

inputs = [
  "Please look up order A-1001.",
  "Please lookup order A-1001.",
  "Can you find order A-1001?",
  "When will my refund post?",
  "Compare a refund, a reship, and a coupon for this late order.",
  "Combine the shipping and refund policies into one answer.",
  "Improve this reply to the customer.",
  "The customer says the box arrived damaged and the item inside is missing a part, what should we tell them?"
]

for input <- inputs do
  {:ok, %{strategy: strategy}} = Select.run(%{input: input}, %{})
  {input, strategy}
end
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {"Please look up order A-1001.", :chain_of_draft},
  {"Please lookup order A-1001.", :react},
  {"Can you find order A-1001?", :react},
  {"When will my refund post?", :chain_of_draft},
  {"Compare a refund, a reship, and a coupon for this late order.", :tree_of_thoughts},
  {"Combine the shipping and refund policies into one answer.", :graph_of_thoughts},
  {"Improve this reply to the customer.", :trm},
  {"The customer says the box arrived damaged and the item inside is missing a part, what should we tell them?",
   :chain_of_thought}
]
```

"look up" is two words, so the first input goes to Chain of Draft and can't
call `lookup_order`. When a request must use a tool, set `strategy: :react`.

## Offline Model

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LLM do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 512,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, __MODULE__}]
    }
  end

  def object(conn, object), do: text(conn, Jason.encode!(object))

  def text(conn, text) do
    respond(conn, %{
      "type" => "message",
      "id" => "msg_1",
      "role" => "assistant",
      "status" => "completed",
      "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
    })
  end

  def tool_call(conn, id, name, arguments) do
    respond(conn, %{
      "type" => "function_call",
      "id" => "fc_" <> id,
      "call_id" => id,
      "name" => name,
      "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
      "status" => "completed"
    })
  end

  defp respond(conn, output) do
    Req.Test.json(conn, %{
      "id" => "resp_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => [output],
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 40,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 10,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 50
      }
    })
  end
end
```

`ask` runs one request on a fresh Agent:

```elixir
ask = fn params ->
  agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
  signal = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(Map.put(params, :options, MyApp.LLM.options()))

  case MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal) do
    {:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} -> reply
    {:error, error} -> {:error, error}
  end
end
```

## Run Selected Flows

"lookup" selects ReAct, which runs the tool and answers:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.tool_call(conn, "call_1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
end)

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "Order A-1001 has shipped and arrives 2026-11-02.")
end)

reply = ask.(%{input: "Please lookup order A-1001."})

{reply.data.flow, reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.tool_rounds}
```

The result is
`{%{id: "jido.ai/reasoning/react", vsn: 1}, "Order A-1001 has shipped and arrives 2026-11-02.", 2, 1}`.

A short policy question selects Chain of Draft, which expects `drafts` and
`answer`:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.object(conn, %{
    drafts: ["refund approved", "card: 5 business days"],
    answer: "Refunds post to your card within 5 business days."
  })
end)

reply = ask.(%{input: "When will my refund post?"})

{reply.data.flow, reply.data.output, reply.data.reasoning.method}
```

The result is
`{%{id: "jido.ai/reasoning/chain-of-draft", vsn: 1}, "Refunds post to your card within 5 business days.", :chain_of_draft}`.

## Override the Flow

`strategy` skips the rule. It accepts `:react`, `:chain_of_thought`,
`:chain_of_draft`, `:algorithm_of_thoughts`, `:tree_of_thoughts`,
`:graph_of_thoughts`, `:trm`, or `nil` (default, use the rule):

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.object(conn, %{
    steps: [
      %{number: 1, content: "The customer wants to know where order A-1001 is."},
      %{number: 2, content: "No tool is available in this Flow, so ask for tracking."}
    ],
    conclusion: "Please share your tracking number so we can check order A-1001."
  })
end)

reply = ask.(%{input: "Please lookup order A-1001.", strategy: :chain_of_thought})

{reply.data.flow, reply.data.reasoning.steps_count}
```

The result is `{%{id: "jido.ai/reasoning/chain-of-thought", vsn: 1}, 2}`.
An atom outside the list fails Adaptive's input validation.

To pin a strategy for every request, set it in the Agent's `reasoning` block
(`strategy :react`) or, better, select that Flow directly with
`reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct`.

## Pass-Through Inputs

`Select` removes `strategy` and passes every other key to the selected Flow.
Adaptive's own schema marks the common fields optional and keeps unknown keys,
so each selected Flow applies its own defaults and its own validation.

| Key | Adaptive validates | Selected Flow |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input` | Required, string or list of parts | Same |
| `instructions`, `model`, `options` | Type only, no default | Applies its default when absent |
| `prompt_cache`, `context` | Shape, when present | Applies its default when absent |
| `limits` | `elapsed_ms` and token or cost budgets; keeps other keys | Rejects keys it doesn't define |
| Other keys | Kept | ReAct accepts its own fields; every Flow rejects unknown keys |

A ReAct-only field works when ReAct is selected and fails for any other Flow.
No model call happens:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  ask.(%{input: "When will my refund post?", limits: %{tool_rounds: 2}})

{error.__struct__, Exception.message(error)}
```

The result is
`{Jido.Action.Error.InvalidInputError, "unrecognized key: tool_rounds, at limits"}`,
and Jido logs a warning for the failed node. Send ReAct fields such as `fallback_models`,
`tool_policy`, `review_tools`, `stream`, `output_schema`, and
`limits.tool_rounds` only together with `strategy: :react`.

Each Flow's `instructions` default also asks for its own output format. If
you set `instructions` on an Adaptive Agent, every selected Flow gets the same
text, so the structured Flows lose their format request while their JSON
schema still applies.

## Summary

- Adaptive is one `invoke`: `Select` picks a strategy, `Expand` runs that
  Flow. The reply is the selected Flow's reply.
- The rule is local and ordered: TRM, Graph of Thoughts, ReAct, Algorithm of
  Thoughts, Tree of Thoughts, then Chain of Draft for fewer than 12 words,
  else Chain of Thought.
- Words match whole, after splitting on characters other than `a-z`, `0-9`,
  and `-`.
- `strategy` overrides the rule per request.
- Other inputs pass through and are validated by the selected Flow.

